Within the weakly-coupled ten-dimensional heterotic string compactified to four dimensions with N=1 supersymmetry, the tree-level relation between the unified gauge coupling g_string, the four-dimensional Newton constant G_N, and the string tension predicts a fundamental unification scale M_string ≈ g_string · 5 × 10^17 GeV at which all gauge couplings and gravity unify. Independently, extrapolating the measured Standard Model gauge couplings upward using the renormalization-group equations of the Minimal Supersymmetric Standard Model (MSSM), with supersymmetric thresholds near the TeV scale and the canonical hypercharge normalization k_1 = 5/3, gives an apparent unification of the three gauge couplings at M_GUT ≈ 2 × 10^16 GeV — a factor of roughly 20 below M_string.

Problem. Construct, or determine the obstructions to constructing, a realistic four-dimensional supersymmetric string-derived model that simultaneously:
(i) reproduces the measured low-energy gauge couplings α_1(M_Z), α_2(M_Z), α_3(M_Z) and the observed Standard Model matter content;
(ii) is consistent with the heterotic prediction for M_string and with its tree-level relation to the gauge and gravitational couplings; and
(iii) achieves agreement between the string unification scale and the effective MSSM unification scale through one or a combination of the following mechanisms, with the chosen mechanism shown to be internally consistent and phenomenologically viable:
  (a) embedding the Standard Model in a string-realized grand unified gauge group (string GUT);
  (b) use of non-standard Kac–Moody affine levels k_i and/or a non-canonical hypercharge normalization;
  (c) heavy string threshold corrections from massive string states;
  (d) light supersymmetric threshold corrections from a non-minimal sparticle spectrum;
  (e) intermediate-scale gauge structure beyond the MSSM between M_Z and M_string;
  (f) additional exotic matter representations charged under the Standard Model gauge group;
  (g) genuinely non-perturbative or strongly-coupled string effects (e.g. M-theory limits).

Identify which of (a)–(g), individually or in combination, can be realized in an explicit string compactification consistent with (i) and (ii), and characterize the resulting low-energy predictions.

